\description{
\code{sigrecon} bundles four small, real-data recontextualization demos
(one per major dataset covered by \verb{scripts/}), each a
\verb{demo_<dataset>_se} / \verb{demo_<dataset>_sigs} / \verb{demo_<dataset>_true_sigs}
triple with the same shape: \verb{demo_<dataset>_se} is target-context
expression, \verb{demo_<dataset>_sigs} are real source-context signatures
(as if defined in a different biological context), and
\verb{demo_<dataset>_true_sigs} is the real target-context ground truth,
for benchmarking recontextualization quality with \code{sig_eval_table()}.
Gene sets are restricted to the union of genes needed by the kept
signatures (not variance-based selection -- see
\code{data-raw/demo_sciplex.R} for why), and perturbations are sampled down
to ~23 for speed. All are fast, self-contained examples for
\code{projectCor()}, \code{wgcna.adj()}/\code{network_sig()}, and \code{sig_eval_table()}
-- see the package README.

No data leakage: each \verb{demo_<dataset>_se}'s perturbation samples are a
set of perturbations DISJOINT from the ~23 evaluated in the
corresponding \verb{_sigs}/\verb{_true_sigs} (control samples plus a \emph{different}
set of ~23 "background" perturbations, not the evaluated ones) -- so
the network/projection input never contains a sample for the
perturbation being predicted. See the "No data leakage" note in each
\code{data-raw/demo_*.R} script.\tabular{llll}{
